#!/usr/bin/env etcs
#IMPORT ACE_ROOT/modules

# A LAYER WINDOW, and like the toolbar it is a script rather than a widget.
#
#     run paint_layers.etcs panel=<Drawable2D> doc=<PaintDocument> list=<PaintLayerPanel>
#
# EVERY ROW IS ORDINARY RECTANGLES, A RASTER AND A LABEL in the caller's 2D
# tree, which is the same bargain paint_toolbar.etcs strikes: PaintProvider
# owns no raster backend and grows none for a list of names. RenderProvider's
# shape, compositor and text leaves already know how to draw one, where it is
# and whether a point is on it; what PaintProvider adds is the only part that
# is about layers -- this node means that row, that part of it means that
# action -- and PaintLayerPanel is that mapping and nothing else.
#
# So the appearance of the window is a file you can edit. Restyling it, making
# the rows taller, dropping the delete button or moving the thumbnail is an
# edit here and to no C++ at all.
#
# A ROW IS [eye][thumb][name ........][x], five nodes for five questions:
# show or hide it, WHICH layer is this (the thumbnail is the one answer that
# does not depend on somebody having named things well), what is it called and
# rename it, take it out, and -- the rectangle under all of them -- draw on
# this one.
#
# THE THUMBNAILS ARE RETAINED COMPOSITORS. PaintLayerPanel writes the layer's
# own pixels into them scaled down (paint_thumb), reaching them as Pixels_ by
# RID exactly as the lifted selection is drawn; SetRetain(1) is what stops the
# next recompose clearing what was written, and they are given no children for
# the same reason the colour wheel's pane has none: one writer per buffer.
#
# WHY THERE IS A ROW COUNT AT ALL. Not a cap on layers: a window shows the ones
# NEAR the one you are looking at, a neighbourhood in the stack's own order
# (ontology/Layer.h), and scrolling moves the frame of reference while these
# same rows are re-bound: the wheel over the window steps it a row per notch
# (PaintInput::RouteEvent hands the notch to PaintLayerPanel::Scroll instead of
# the zoom), up toward the top of the stack, clamped at both ends. The count
# is the resident set, which is what the window's height divided by the row
# height already was. The bytes behind the
# churn are the arena's and are already recycled -- every ArenaAllocator-backed
# container returns exact-size slots to its scope's free list -- so nothing
# here pools anything.
#
# NAMES NO ABSOLUTE POSITION, like every component here: each node is placed in
# `panel`'s space, so moving the panel moves the window with nothing here
# recomputed, and the input that finds these nodes arrives already translated
# into the same space they were laid out in (Drawable2D_::PickAt). That is
# also what makes the title bar a handle: dragging it moves `panel`
# (PaintLayerPanel::BindTitle) and every row comes along.
#
# NO CLOSE BUTTON, on purpose. Rows have a delete; the window does not, since a
# window that can be dismissed is one that will be, and there is nothing else
# that shows which layer the brush is on. The base row has no delete either --
# the document refuses that anyway (PaintDocument::RemoveLayer) and the panel
# hides the button rather than draw one that only ever says no.

requires panel [Drawable2D]
requires doc   [PaintDocument]
requires list  [PaintLayerPanel]

list.Create()
list.BindDocument(@doc)

# The look, stated once. Eye colours are shown/hidden; row colours are
# selected/idle. The hover dim is how far every OTHER layer recedes while a row
# is under the pointer -- 0.25 rather than 0 so the rest of the picture stays
# legible as context, since isolating a layer is a way of finding it.
#
# ── THE PANEL BELONGS TO THE RULER, NOT TO THE CANVAS ───────────────────────
#
# Every value below is taken from the edge ruler rather than invented: the
# panel's ground is the ruler's own band (0.106, 0.110, 0.078 -- PaintSurface's
# m_ruler_bg), its ink is the ruler's ink (0.94, 0.89, 0.78 -- m_ruler_ink), and
# the selected row is the ruler's in-band highlight (0.35, 0.55, 0.95, the
# colour a tick takes inside the page).
#
# Not a neutral grey-blue a shade off the canvas background, which is the one
# thing it should NOT match: the canvas is the PICTURE, and a panel the colour
# of the picture reads as part of it. The ruler is the other furniture in this
# window -- the frame around the page -- and furniture should look like
# furniture. With the panel and the ruler the same two colours the eye groups
# them, and neither competes with what is being drawn.
#
# The shown eye is the ruler's ink at full strength and the hidden one is that
# same ink sunk most of the way into the band, so "off" is the same colour
# turned down rather than a second, greyer one.
list.SetEyeColors(0.94, 0.89, 0.78, 0.35, 0.36, 0.28)
# The iris: the page's highlight while the eye is open, the band while shut.
list.SetIrisColors(0.35, 0.55, 0.95, 0.106, 0.110, 0.078)
list.SetRowColors(0.35, 0.55, 0.95, 0.15, 0.16, 0.11)
list.SetHoverDim(0.25)

# ── the frame ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A backing rectangle rather than a background on the composite, so the window
# reads as a panel with an edge even before it has any rows. Order 0: every row
# sits above it.

panel.spawn(RenderProvider::PolygonDrawable2D layers_back)
layers_back.Create()
layers_back.AddPoint(0, 0)
layers_back.AddPoint(240, 0)
layers_back.AddPoint(240, 224)
layers_back.AddPoint(0, 224)
# The ruler's own band (PaintSurface::m_ruler_bg), exactly.
layers_back.SetFill(0.106, 0.110, 0.078, 1.0)
layers_back.SetOrder(0)

# ── the title bar, which is the handle, and the + on it ──────────────────
#
# The bar and the word on it are both bound, so a press on either drags the
# window; the pane itself is what moves (BindWindow). The + is not a handle: it
# is bound separately (BindAdd) and adds a layer above the active one, so
# pressing it cannot also start a drag.

panel.spawn(RenderProvider::PolygonDrawable2D layers_title)
layers_title.Create()
layers_title.AddPoint(0, 0)
layers_title.AddPoint(240, 0)
layers_title.AddPoint(240, 22)
layers_title.AddPoint(0, 22)
# One step up from the band rather than a different colour: a title bar is
# the same material, lit.
layers_title.SetFill(0.17, 0.18, 0.13, 1.0)
layers_title.SetOrder(1)

panel.spawn(RenderProvider::TextLabel layers_title_word)
layers_title_word.Create()
layers_title_word.SetPosition(8, 7)
layers_title_word.SetSize(1)
layers_title_word.SetColor(0.94, 0.89, 0.78, 1.0)
layers_title_word.SetText(layers)
layers_title_word.SetOrder(2)

# ── the view toggle, beside the +: AN EYE ────────────────────────────────────
#
# COLLAPSES EVERYTHING BUT THE BAR, and pressing it again brings the list back.
# Not a close: a window with a cross on it is one somebody shuts by accident and
# then cannot reopen, and the bar left on screen is both the way back and the
# reminder that there is one (PaintLayerPanel::BindView says the same).
#
# It is the same eye the rows have, from the same file, because it means the
# same thing here -- shown or not -- and a "-" meant nothing: open while the
# rows show, shut while the window is its bar. paint_eye.etcs draws at (8..30,
# 8..18) in the pane it is given, so a small pane puts it beside the +; run
# between BeginTitle and the first BeginRow, its two nodes register as the view
# toggle rather than as a row's (PaintLayerPanel::RowNode).
panel.spawn(RenderProvider::CompositeDrawable2D layers_view)
layers_view.Create(38, 26)
layers_view.SetPosition(182, -2)
layers_view.SetOrder(2)
list.BeginTitle()
run paint_eye.etcs pane=layers_view list=list

panel.spawn(RenderProvider::PolygonDrawable2D layers_add)
layers_add.Create()
layers_add.AddPoint(214, 3)
layers_add.AddPoint(234, 3)
layers_add.AddPoint(234, 19)
layers_add.AddPoint(214, 19)
# The + keeps its green, pulled into the band's hue so it reads as a control
# on this panel rather than a swatch borrowed from somewhere else.
layers_add.SetFill(0.24, 0.33, 0.19, 1.0)
layers_add.SetOrder(2)

panel.spawn(RenderProvider::TextLabel layers_add_word)
layers_add_word.Create()
layers_add_word.SetPosition(221, 7)
layers_add_word.SetSize(1)
layers_add_word.SetColor(0.94, 0.89, 0.78, 1.0)
layers_add_word.SetText(+)
layers_add_word.SetOrder(3)

list.BindTitle(@layers_title)
list.BindTitle(@layers_title_word)
list.BindWindow(@panel)
list.BindAdd(@layers_add)
list.BindAdd(@layers_add_word)
# The plate goes with the rows when the window collapses to its bar.
list.BindBody(@layers_back)

# ── the rows ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Seven rows of 28 under the bar, top of the window first -- the order BeginRow
# opens them in, and the order somebody reading this file sees them on screen.
#
#
#
# SEVEN PANES, ONE SCRIPT. Each row is a pane this file positions and
# paint_layer_row.etcs fills in -- which is why that file can exist at all: a
# script cannot be told a number (`run` carries RIDs), but it can be handed a
# pane, and a pane is a coordinate origin. What used to be ninety-six lines
# seven times is now four lines seven times.
#
# THE PANE IS THE ROW'S PLATE. It is a compositor with a colour, and the
# panel's fill helper already knows a composite takes SetBackground where a
# polygon takes SetFill -- so the background and the row are one node.

panel.spawn(RenderProvider::CompositeDrawable2D row0)
row0.Create(232, 26)
row0.SetPosition(4, 26)
row0.SetOrder(1)
row0.SetBackground(0.15, 0.16, 0.11, 1.0)
#
# NAMED AS A SIBLING, not by its path from the module root, and that is not a
# style choice. resolve_script_path tries the caller's OWN directory first and
# falls back to the caller's #IMPORT target; a nested run spelled
# "PaintProvider/scripts/x.etcs" from inside PaintProvider/scripts/ asks for
# PaintProvider/scripts/PaintProvider/scripts/x.etcs, misses, and is saved only
# by the #IMPORT fallback -- which exists natively and does NOT in the browser,
# where the scripts are staged into MEMFS at their relative paths and ACE_ROOT
# means nothing. Spelled as a sibling it resolves on the FIRST candidate in both
# places. A top-level run from the boot script still names the full path,
# because from there it is not a sibling.
run paint_layer_row.etcs pane=row0 list=list

panel.spawn(RenderProvider::CompositeDrawable2D row1)
row1.Create(232, 26)
row1.SetPosition(4, 54)
row1.SetOrder(1)
row1.SetBackground(0.15, 0.16, 0.11, 1.0)
run paint_layer_row.etcs pane=row1 list=list

panel.spawn(RenderProvider::CompositeDrawable2D row2)
row2.Create(232, 26)
row2.SetPosition(4, 82)
row2.SetOrder(1)
row2.SetBackground(0.15, 0.16, 0.11, 1.0)
run paint_layer_row.etcs pane=row2 list=list

panel.spawn(RenderProvider::CompositeDrawable2D row3)
row3.Create(232, 26)
row3.SetPosition(4, 110)
row3.SetOrder(1)
row3.SetBackground(0.15, 0.16, 0.11, 1.0)
run paint_layer_row.etcs pane=row3 list=list

panel.spawn(RenderProvider::CompositeDrawable2D row4)
row4.Create(232, 26)
row4.SetPosition(4, 138)
row4.SetOrder(1)
row4.SetBackground(0.15, 0.16, 0.11, 1.0)
run paint_layer_row.etcs pane=row4 list=list

panel.spawn(RenderProvider::CompositeDrawable2D row5)
row5.Create(232, 26)
row5.SetPosition(4, 166)
row5.SetOrder(1)
row5.SetBackground(0.15, 0.16, 0.11, 1.0)
run paint_layer_row.etcs pane=row5 list=list

panel.spawn(RenderProvider::CompositeDrawable2D row6)
row6.Create(232, 26)
row6.SetPosition(4, 194)
row6.SetOrder(1)
row6.SetBackground(0.15, 0.16, 0.11, 1.0)
run paint_layer_row.etcs pane=row6 list=list


# Bind the stack to the rows now that all of them exist. Called again by the
# panel itself after anything that changes what the window should say, so a
# script only has to say it once.
list.Refresh()
